1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,648
Welcome to episode eleven.

2
00:00:01,688 --> 00:00:05,933
Real calorimeters lose heat, and
real thermometers aren't perfect.

3
00:00:05,973 --> 00:00:08,855
Today we'll learn to
read temperature graphs,

4
00:00:08,895 --> 00:00:12,361
correct for heat lost while
the reaction is happening,

5
00:00:12,401 --> 00:00:14,609
and reason carefully about errors.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,145
Two quick checks.

7
00:00:16,185 --> 00:00:19,560
If heat escapes during an
electrical calibration,

8
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:23,742
is the calibration factor
calculated too large or too small?

9
00:00:23,782 --> 00:00:27,575
And if a reaction raises the
calorimeter's temperature,

10
00:00:27,615 --> 00:00:29,805
is delta H positive or negative?

11
00:00:29,845 --> 00:00:31,030
Pause and answer.

12
00:00:39,467 --> 00:00:44,981
Too large: the measured rise is
smaller, and C F equals E over delta T.

13
00:00:45,021 --> 00:00:51,593
And delta H is negative, because the reaction
transferred energy to the calorimeter.

14
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:56,811
Here's real-looking data, from
practice question Q twenty-one.

15
00:00:56,851 --> 00:01:00,711
Temperature is on the vertical
axis, in degrees Celsius,

16
00:01:00,751 --> 00:01:03,984
and time is on the
horizontal axis, in seconds.

17
00:01:04,024 --> 00:01:09,805
Each point is an actual measurement; we
won't draw a decorative curve through them.

18
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:15,150
Before mixing, the temperature is steady
at twenty-one point zero degrees.

19
00:01:15,190 --> 00:01:16,499
That's the baseline.

20
00:01:16,539 --> 00:01:19,843
Mixing happens at one
hundred and twenty seconds.

21
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:24,294
After mixing, the temperature climbs
quickly: twenty-three, twenty-five,

22
00:01:24,334 --> 00:01:28,084
twenty-six, and the highest reading,
twenty-six point one degrees,

23
00:01:28,124 --> 00:01:30,077
at one hundred and eighty seconds.

24
00:01:30,466 --> 00:01:35,491
After that, the readings fall slowly
and steadily: twenty-five point nine,

25
00:01:35,531 --> 00:01:38,776
twenty-five point seven,
twenty-five point five.

26
00:01:38,816 --> 00:01:41,719
The calorimeter is
losing heat to the room.

27
00:01:41,759 --> 00:01:44,428
That steady fall is the cooling region.

28
00:01:45,399 --> 00:01:46,783
Here's the problem.

29
00:01:46,823 --> 00:01:52,178
The calorimeter starts losing heat as
soon as it's warmer than the room,

30
00:01:52,218 --> 00:01:55,700
which is while the reaction
is still happening.

31
00:01:55,740 --> 00:02:00,836
So by the time the temperature peaks,
some heat has already escaped.

32
00:02:01,199 --> 00:02:06,187
That means the highest measured temperature
is lower than the temperature the

33
00:02:06,227 --> 00:02:11,476
calorimeter would have reached if the
reaction had been instantaneous and no heat

34
00:02:11,516 --> 00:02:12,259
had escaped.

35
00:02:12,299 --> 00:02:15,524
Using it would underestimate
the temperature rise,

36
00:02:15,564 --> 00:02:18,111
and so underestimate the heat released.

37
00:02:19,065 --> 00:02:24,155
To estimate what the temperature
would have been without that loss,

38
00:02:24,195 --> 00:02:26,146
we use the cooling region.

39
00:02:26,186 --> 00:02:30,357
Draw the best straight line
through the cooling points.

40
00:02:30,397 --> 00:02:32,847
Here they lie exactly on a line.

41
00:02:33,199 --> 00:02:38,666
The slope: the temperature falls from
twenty-six point one to twenty-five point

42
00:02:38,706 --> 00:02:42,081
five degrees over one
hundred and eighty seconds.

43
00:02:42,121 --> 00:02:46,194
That's negative zero point six
over one hundred and eighty,

44
00:02:46,234 --> 00:02:51,532
or about negative zero point zero zero
three three three degrees per second.

45
00:02:51,865 --> 00:02:55,408
Now extend that line
backwards, as a dashed line,

46
00:02:55,448 --> 00:02:59,649
to the moment of mixing at one
hundred and twenty seconds.

47
00:02:59,689 --> 00:03:05,572
Going back sixty seconds adds sixty times
zero point zero zero three three three,

48
00:03:05,612 --> 00:03:07,912
which is zero point two degrees.

49
00:03:07,952 --> 00:03:13,509
So the extrapolated temperature at mixing
is twenty-six point three degrees.

50
00:03:13,865 --> 00:03:15,345
Compare the two rises.

51
00:03:15,385 --> 00:03:17,764
Using the highest observed reading:

52
00:03:17,804 --> 00:03:23,430
twenty-six point one minus twenty-one point
zero, which is five point one degrees.

53
00:03:23,470 --> 00:03:29,026
Using the extrapolated value: twenty-six
point three minus twenty-one point zero,

54
00:03:29,066 --> 00:03:31,376
which is five point three degrees.

55
00:03:31,416 --> 00:03:37,110
The corrected rise is larger, because it
adds back the heat lost while the reaction

56
00:03:37,150 --> 00:03:38,118
was happening.

57
00:03:38,465 --> 00:03:42,769
Treat the extrapolated value
as an estimate from a model.

58
00:03:42,809 --> 00:03:46,046
It assumes the cooling
rate stays the same,

59
00:03:46,086 --> 00:03:50,237
and that the reaction happened
at the moment of mixing.

60
00:03:50,277 --> 00:03:53,591
It's a better estimate
than the raw maximum,

61
00:03:53,631 --> 00:03:57,212
not a perfect recovery
of the true temperature.

62
00:03:58,198 --> 00:04:01,969
Practice question Q twenty-one
is worth five marks.

63
00:04:02,009 --> 00:04:07,014
You've seen the graph; pause the
video and write the full solution.

64
00:04:19,465 --> 00:04:24,900
Part a: the cooling slope is negative
zero point zero zero three three three

65
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:30,592
degrees per second, and extrapolating
back sixty seconds gives twenty-six point

66
00:04:30,632 --> 00:04:32,321
three degrees at mixing.

67
00:04:32,361 --> 00:04:36,323
Part b: the corrected rise
is five point three degrees.

68
00:04:36,665 --> 00:04:42,017
Part c: the corrected heat is six
hundred and fifty times five point three:

69
00:04:42,057 --> 00:04:45,612
three thousand four hundred
and forty-five joules.

70
00:04:45,652 --> 00:04:50,932
Since the temperature rise is a graph
estimate to two significant figures,

71
00:04:50,972 --> 00:04:54,351
report it as about three
point four kilojoules.

72
00:04:54,732 --> 00:05:00,206
Part d: the highest observed temperature
gives a rise of five point one degrees,

73
00:05:00,246 --> 00:05:05,858
and six hundred and fifty times five point
one is three thousand three hundred and

74
00:05:05,898 --> 00:05:09,373
fifteen joules, about three
point three kilojoules.

75
00:05:09,413 --> 00:05:14,887
That's an underestimate, because cooling
had already started before the peak was

76
00:05:14,927 --> 00:05:15,727
recorded.

77
00:05:15,932 --> 00:05:21,550
Marks: two for the correct region and
extrapolation, one for the corrected rise,

78
00:05:21,590 --> 00:05:26,784
one for the corrected heat, and one
for the comparison with its direction.

79
00:05:26,824 --> 00:05:31,169
The traps: using the observed
maximum as if it were corrected,

80
00:05:31,209 --> 00:05:36,514
or reading twenty-six point three
degrees itself as the temperature change.

81
00:05:37,465 --> 00:05:43,489
Heat can escape at two different stages,
and the effects go in different directions.

82
00:05:43,529 --> 00:05:48,583
That's why "heat loss makes the answer
smaller" isn't a reliable rule.

83
00:05:48,931 --> 00:05:53,308
During calibration, the electrical
energy is known correctly.

84
00:05:53,348 --> 00:05:57,797
Heat loss makes the measured
temperature rise smaller, so C F,

85
00:05:57,837 --> 00:06:00,878
which is E over delta T, comes out larger.

86
00:06:01,265 --> 00:06:05,596
During the reaction, with a
valid calibration factor fixed,

87
00:06:05,636 --> 00:06:09,597
uncorrected heat loss makes
the measured rise smaller.

88
00:06:09,637 --> 00:06:15,525
Then q, which is C F times delta T, is
smaller, so the calculated heat released,

89
00:06:15,565 --> 00:06:19,418
and the size of the molar
enthalpy, are smaller too.

90
00:06:19,798 --> 00:06:24,021
Same physical cause, opposite
effects on the calculated quantity,

91
00:06:24,061 --> 00:06:28,940
because the temperature rise sits in
the denominator in one formula and the

92
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:30,449
numerator in the other.

93
00:06:30,489 --> 00:06:34,227
Always follow the measurement
through the actual formula.

94
00:06:35,198 --> 00:06:37,159
Some vocabulary, used precisely.

95
00:06:37,199 --> 00:06:41,224
A random error makes repeated
measurements scatter unpredictably,

96
00:06:41,264 --> 00:06:43,287
sometimes high and sometimes low.

97
00:06:43,327 --> 00:06:48,477
A systematic error pushes every result in
the same direction by a consistent amount

98
00:06:48,517 --> 00:06:49,393
or proportion.

99
00:06:49,731 --> 00:06:53,508
Accuracy is how close a
result is to the true value.

100
00:06:53,548 --> 00:06:58,721
Precision describes how closely
repeated results agree with each other.

101
00:06:58,761 --> 00:07:03,786
Here's a set of results that are
tightly grouped, so they're precise,

102
00:07:03,826 --> 00:07:07,383
but they're all too low,
so they're not accurate.

103
00:07:07,423 --> 00:07:10,727
That's the fingerprint
of a systematic error.

104
00:07:11,064 --> 00:07:13,168
Repeatability means the same person,

105
00:07:13,208 --> 00:07:18,050
method and equipment get closely agreeing
results when the experiment is repeated.

106
00:07:18,090 --> 00:07:21,980
Good repeatability shows precision,
but it doesn't prove accuracy.

107
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:26,327
Validity asks whether the experiment
actually measures what it claims to,

108
00:07:26,367 --> 00:07:28,570
under suitable controlled conditions.

109
00:07:28,931 --> 00:07:33,365
Averaging more repeats reduces
the effect of random errors.

110
00:07:33,405 --> 00:07:36,474
It does nothing about a systematic error:

111
00:07:36,514 --> 00:07:40,230
you just get a better
estimate of a biased value.

112
00:07:41,197 --> 00:07:45,006
Practice question Q twenty-two
is worth five marks.

113
00:07:45,046 --> 00:07:47,764
Pause the video and work through it.

114
00:08:03,197 --> 00:08:03,665
Part a.

115
00:08:03,705 --> 00:08:06,861
The spreadsheet uses
four hundred and fifty:

116
00:08:06,901 --> 00:08:12,598
four hundred and fifty times four point
zero zero is one thousand eight hundred

117
00:08:12,638 --> 00:08:18,263
joules, divided by zero point zero four
zero zero moles is forty-five thousand

118
00:08:18,303 --> 00:08:19,425
joules per mole.

119
00:08:19,465 --> 00:08:24,945
So the reported value is negative
forty-five point zero kilojoules per mole.

120
00:08:24,985 --> 00:08:29,376
With the valid five hundred,
the heat is two thousand joules,

121
00:08:29,416 --> 00:08:34,717
and the corrected value is negative
fifty point zero kilojoules per mole.

122
00:08:35,063 --> 00:08:35,515
Part b.

123
00:08:35,555 --> 00:08:38,462
Repeating the experiment doesn't fix this.

124
00:08:38,502 --> 00:08:42,111
Every repeat uses the same
wrong calibration factor,

125
00:08:42,151 --> 00:08:46,672
so the results agree closely with
each other: good repeatability.

126
00:08:46,712 --> 00:08:51,274
But they share the same systematic
bias, so they stay inaccurate.

127
00:08:51,663 --> 00:08:52,175
Part c.

128
00:08:52,215 --> 00:08:58,084
A thermometer that reads one point five
degrees too high, on both readings,

129
00:08:58,124 --> 00:09:03,126
adds one point five to the initial
and to the final temperature.

130
00:09:03,166 --> 00:09:09,350
The difference is final plus one point
five, minus initial plus one point five,

131
00:09:09,390 --> 00:09:11,792
and the one point fives cancel.

132
00:09:11,832 --> 00:09:13,487
Delta T is unchanged.

133
00:09:13,863 --> 00:09:15,539
But don't over-generalise.

134
00:09:15,579 --> 00:09:19,631
That cancellation only works for
an identical additive offset.

135
00:09:19,671 --> 00:09:24,977
A thermometer whose scale is stretched,
reading too many degrees per real degree,

136
00:09:25,017 --> 00:09:26,403
would change delta T.

137
00:09:26,797 --> 00:09:31,078
Marks: two for the two enthalpies,
one for the repetition explanation,

138
00:09:31,118 --> 00:09:35,090
and two for the offset conclusion
with the subtraction reasoning.

139
00:09:35,130 --> 00:09:38,053
The traps: equating
repeatability with accuracy,

140
00:09:38,093 --> 00:09:42,413
and assuming every thermometer error
changes a temperature difference.

141
00:09:43,396 --> 00:09:45,083
Here's a summary table.

142
00:09:45,123 --> 00:09:48,613
Each row follows one
fault through the formula,

143
00:09:48,653 --> 00:09:52,859
under the simple model where
only that fault is present.

144
00:09:53,196 --> 00:09:57,500
Heat loss during electrical
calibration, with E known correctly:

145
00:09:57,540 --> 00:10:02,524
the measured rise goes down, so the
calculated calibration factor goes up.

146
00:10:02,564 --> 00:10:07,615
Uncorrected heat loss during the
reaction, with a valid calibration factor:

147
00:10:07,655 --> 00:10:12,950
the measured rise goes down, so the
inferred heat, and its magnitude, go down.

148
00:10:13,329 --> 00:10:15,998
A calibration factor that's too small:

149
00:10:16,038 --> 00:10:20,845
the calculated heat and the magnitude
of the molar enthalpy go down.

150
00:10:20,885 --> 00:10:24,695
The same additive thermometer
offset on both readings:

151
00:10:24,735 --> 00:10:27,546
the temperature difference is unchanged.

152
00:10:27,586 --> 00:10:31,395
And more repeat trials with
the same systematic fault:

153
00:10:31,435 --> 00:10:35,926
a better estimate of a biased
mean, with the fault still there.

154
00:10:36,262 --> 00:10:37,183
One caution.

155
00:10:37,223 --> 00:10:42,466
If several faults act together,
the net effect needs a full model.

156
00:10:42,506 --> 00:10:45,108
They can partly cancel or add up,

157
00:10:45,148 --> 00:10:49,630
so the table alone can't tell
you the overall direction.

158
00:10:49,996 --> 00:10:55,789
And when an exam asks for an improvement,
be specific: insulate the lid and sides,

159
00:10:55,829 --> 00:11:01,267
calibrate with the same volume of solution
that will be used in the reaction,

160
00:11:01,307 --> 00:11:04,610
record temperatures more
often and extrapolate,

161
00:11:04,650 --> 00:11:08,563
or repeat with fresh solutions
and average the results.

162
00:11:09,529 --> 00:11:10,127
To recap.

163
00:11:10,167 --> 00:11:14,239
Read the graph's baseline,
mixing time and cooling region.

164
00:11:14,279 --> 00:11:19,486
Extend the cooling trend back to the
mixing time to estimate the corrected

165
00:11:19,526 --> 00:11:23,172
temperature, and treat it
as a model-based estimate.

166
00:11:23,212 --> 00:11:27,356
Trace every error through the
actual formula, and remember:

167
00:11:27,396 --> 00:11:29,877
precise isn't the same as accurate.

168
00:11:30,262 --> 00:11:31,316
Closing recall.

169
00:11:31,356 --> 00:11:37,368
A cooling line has a slope of negative zero
point zero zero two degrees per second.

170
00:11:37,408 --> 00:11:41,160
Extrapolating back fifty
seconds to the mixing time,

171
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:44,043
how much does the temperature increase?

172
00:11:54,462 --> 00:12:00,139
Fifty seconds times zero point zero zero
two degrees per second is zero point one

173
00:12:00,179 --> 00:12:03,526
degrees, added to the
start of the cooling line.

174
00:12:03,566 --> 00:12:09,071
Next episode: comparing fuels fairly,
and what sustainability really requires.
